/******************************************************************************
* djinterp [re_std]                                                     cstdint
*
* <cstdint> umbrella header:
*   Aggregates the granular headers under /inc/djinterp/re_std/cstdint/.
* Mirrors the standard <cstdint> convenience header and adds no symbols of
* its own.
*
*   THE MACRO HALF OF THIS HEADER HAS NO re_std:: SPELLING:
*   <cstdint> is two things -- a set of typedefs and a set of macros. The
* typedefs are re-exported into re_std:: by cstdint.hpp. The macros are
* not, because macros have no namespace:
*
*     INT8_MIN / INT8_MAX ... INT64_MAX      exact-width limits
*     INT_LEAST*_MIN / MAX, INT_FAST*_MIN / MAX
*     INTMAX_MIN / INTMAX_MAX, INTPTR_MIN / INTPTR_MAX
*     PTRDIFF_MIN / PTRDIFF_MAX, SIZE_MAX
*     SIG_ATOMIC_MIN / MAX, WCHAR_MIN / MAX, WINT_MIN / MAX
*     INT8_C(v) ... INTMAX_C(v), UINT8_C(v) ... UINTMAX_C(v)
*
*   Including this umbrella makes all of them available exactly as the
* standard header does; no D_RE_STD_ aliases are minted for them, since a
* renamed macro would be a second spelling of the same thing with no
* portability gain.
*
*   PREFER numeric_limits WHERE YOU CAN:
*   re_std::numeric_limits<T>::max() is the typed, namespaced, ADL-safe
* answer to the same question as INT64_MAX, and it is constexpr from
* C++11. The macros remain the right tool in exactly two places: inside
* preprocessor conditionals (#if INTMAX_MAX > ...), where a constexpr
* function cannot be called, and for the INT*_C literal suffixes.
* re_std::ratio's zetta / yotta typedefs are gated on INTMAX_MAX for that
* first reason.
*
*
* path:      /inc/djinterp/re_std/cstdint/cstdint
* link(s):   TBA
* author(s): TBA                                           created: 2026.08.25
******************************************************************************/

